  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nWidely scattered showers are moving across parts of northern IL\nearly this afternoon. These showers have largely formed along \nan elevated baroclinic zone on the leading edge of a warm \nadvective push. The highest concentration of showers is found \nnear and north of I-90 as they approach the Fox Valley as of 2 \nPM. Highest coverage is expected to continue across our far \nnorth and into southern WI through the remainder of the \nafternoon with generally lesser coverage farther south, but most\nof the Chicago metro is in for at least a brief round of light \nrain before the end of the afternoon. There were previously some\nembedded storms as these showers moved across northwest IL, but\nlightning coverage has since retreated up into southern WI and \nthunder chances locally through the afternoon will be low, \nhighest near the WI state line. One exception is a persistent \nnarrow corridor of showers and embedded thunderstorms that has \nbeen stirring near our southwestern CWA border for a couple of \nhours along a defined confluence axis. This feature is expected \nto make little headway toward the northeast into parts of \nLivingston and LaSalle counties as it gradually fizzles away \nthrough mid-afternoon. \n\nThe area should be rain-free for the evening and mostly cloudy \nskies will clear out to the east. Temperatures are expected to \ndrop into the 50s by early tomorrow and stay closer to 60 in and\naround the city. Though we'll be parked beneath a stout \nnocturnal inversion, a low level jet just off the deck with as \nmany as 30 to 35 kt of flow at 975 mb should bring marginally \nbreezy conditions overnight with occasional gusts north of 20 \nmph possible. High-level cloud cover is expected to fill in from\nthe southeast beginning tonight ahead of the remnant hurricane.\n\nNo precip is anticipated tomorrow, but cloud cover will \ncontinue to expand around the area through the day. Tomorrow \nwill be a warm one with highs in the upper 70s and lower 80s and\na southerly breeze. Temperatures on Sunday will range from the \nlower 70s in our east with thicker cloud cover and a chance for \nlight rain to the upper 70s out west toward I-39. Light showers \nwill be possible Saturday night and Sunday morning primarily \neast of I-55, although an isolated shower can't be ruled out as \nfar northwest as I-55 and up into the city.\n\nA broad center of low pressure is favored to track nearby and \ndrag a cold front across the region early next week. \nAccordingly, we find a chance for scattered showers and embedded\nthunderstorms from Monday evening into early Tuesday. Warm \nadvection ahead of the system is expected to bring more \nunseasonably warm temperatures to Monday with highs forecast in \nthe 80s area-wide, including upper 80s possible across the \noutlying areas. More seasonable conditions are then favored \nthrough the rest of the work week. Additional rounds of precip \nare expected in the Wednesday through Friday window with a \nseries of disturbances progged to move across the region.\n\nDoom",
